    "available_at": "2026-11-22T13:42:17.538926+00:00",
    "body": "Borderlands Mexico is a weekly rundown of developments in the world of United States-Mexico cross-border trucking and trade. This week in Borderlands Mexico: Truckers secure 29 agreements on highway safety after nationwide strike; MISUMI Americas expands Mexico operations with new 45,000-square-foot facility; and Prime Fuel Solutions expands Texas fleet, targets neighboring states.\nTruckers secure 29 agreements on highway safety after nationwide strike\nMexico’s federal government and trucking industry representatives have advanced 29 agreements aimed at combating cargo theft, improving highway security and addressing deteriorating infrastructure.\nThe agreements arrive nearly a month after nationwide trucker protests disrupted freight transportation across 19 states.\nThe deals emerged from six working groups involving 10 government authorities and representatives of the National Chamber of Freight Transportation (CANACAR), the Honorable Mexican Alliance of Transport Organizations (HAMOTAC), the Mexican-American Federation of Transporters (FEMATRAC) and the Integrating Organization of Conventional Rights (INDECO).\nThe organizations, supported by Mexico’s Confederation of National Chambers of Commerce, Services and Tourism (CONCANACO SERVYTUR), announced the progress Wednesday, during a news conference in Mexico City.\nThe negotiations focus on persistent problems affecting Mexico’s trucking industry, including cargo theft, extortion, highway maintenance, excessive towing charges, regulatory requirements and the safety of commercial truck drivers.\nThe discussions follow an Oct. 26-27 nationwide trucking strike that prompted demonstrations and highway blockades across major freight corridors.\nMexico’s Interior Ministry reported demonstrations at 38 locations across 19 states, involving 691 vehicles and 858 participants.\nThe protests highlighted concerns about highway crime and rising transportation costs, particularly along routes connecting Mexico’s industrial centers to U.S. border crossings.\nHighway security and cargo theft remain top priorities\nOne of the central components of the negotiations involves improving coordination among Mexico’s Interior Ministry (SEGOB), the Ministry of Security and Citizen Protection (SSPC), the National Guard and state authorities.\nThe agreements call for establishing direct communication channels between transportation companies and law enforcement agencies to report criminal activity and respond to security incidents.\nAuthorities and trucking representatives also agreed to work toward standardizing procedures used by prosecutors, including efforts to accelerate the release of recovered trucks following cargo theft investigations.\nOther measures include identifying unauthorized highway checkpoints and strengthening enforcement against devices used to jam GPS tracking signals.\nThe initiatives are intended to improve the government’s ability to prevent, investigate and prosecute crimes affecting freight transportation companies and their drivers.\nAccording to figures presented during the news conference on Wednesday by Mexican transportation publication T21 , Mexico recorded 6,211 cargo transportation robberies from January through September 2026.\nCANACAR also reported 145 additional incidents during October.\nCarlos García Álvarez, national president of HAMOTAC, expressed frustration that highway crimes have continued despite meetings with federal authorities.\nTrucking groups identify 62 highways needing repairs\nInfrastructure improvements represent another major component of the negotiations.\nThe trucking organizations submitted an assessment to Mexico’s Ministry of Infrastructure, Communications and Transportation (SICT) identifying 62 highway segments requiring priority maintenance and repairs.\nThe assessment is intended to help federal authorities identify roads for inclusion in future construction and maintenance programs.\nIndustry representatives also reported progress toward reclassifying approximately 11,000 kilometers, or 6,835 miles, of Mexico’s highway network.\nThe proposed changes cover 40 segments across 102 highways, with publication in Mexico’s Official Gazette expected by December.\nAdalberto Gutiérrez Muñoz, national president of INDECO, criticized deteriorating highway conditions and the high tolls paid by commercial carriers.\nHe also called for improvements to highway access points that could be exploited by criminal organizations.\nAccording to CANACAR, freight transportation companies contributed more than $3.6 billion, representing approximately 56% of toll highway revenue in 2025.\nGovernment targets towing abuses and regulatory costs\nAnother significant agreement involves the planned December launch of SICT’s computerized towing service registration system, known as SIRSE.\nThe platform is designed to improve transparency in towing, vehicle recovery and impound services while reducing excessive charges and abusive practices.\nTrucking representatives have repeatedly complained about towing costs and the difficulties associated with recovering commercial vehicles following accidents or law enforcement investigations.\nThe organizations also submitted an assessment of federal, state and municipal regulatory requirements to Mexico’s Digital Transformation and Telecommunications Agency.\nThe review could lead to a unified digital record for trucking companies and a single-window system intended to simplify administrative procedures.\nAdditional discussions include proposed tax incentives for private investment in secure truck parking facilities and driver rest areas.\nIndustry representatives are also seeking clarification of forthcoming labor regulations governing electronic work-hour records, including how driving, waiting, loading, unloading and rest periods will be classified.\nMISUMI Americas expands Mexico operations with new 45,000-square-foot facility\nMISUMI Americas has opened a new 45,000-square-foot manufacturing and fulfillment facility in Monterrey, Mexico , more than quadrupling its previous footprint as the company expands its capabilities to serve the country’s growing industrial sector.\nThe Oakland, California-based supplier of mechanical components and assemblies announced the expansion Nov. 16, saying the new facility adds in-house manufacturing and fulfillment of mechanical assemblies while strengthening its network of local manufacturing partners.\nSince establishing its Mexico center of excellence in the Monterrey region in 2023, MISUMI Americas has expanded its Mexican manufacturing partner network by more than 250%, according to a news release.\nThe company’s Mexico operations support customers in aerospace, medical technology, semiconductors, robotics, automotive and energy. Its manufacturing capabilities include CNC machining, sheet metal fabrication, injection molding, extrusions and castings.\n“Demand in Mexico has grown, and this facility is how we keep pace,” Dave Evans, president and CEO of MISUMI Americas, said in a statement. “Bringing assembly in-house and growing our manufacturing partner network gives customers more ways to get their parts and assemblies from one team.”\nPrime Fuel Solutions expands Texas fleet, targets neighboring states\nAbilene, Texas-based Prime Fuel Solutions is expanding its fuel delivery fleet and extending operations into neighboring states as it works toward establishing a nationwide fuel logistics network.\nThe company, which provides on-site fueling services for construction, industrial and other mission-critical projects, announced the expansion on Wednesday, along with the launch of a new website designed to streamline customer access to its services.\nPrime Fuel Solutions said it is adding trucks and drivers to meet growing demand for scheduled and emergency fuel deliveries. The company provides diesel, gasoline, diesel exhaust fluid (DEF) and propane, along with bulk fuel storage, tank rentals and on-site fuel management.\nPrime Fuel Solutions also offers 24-hour emergency fueling and delivery reporting. Its new website, primefuelsolutions.com , allows contractors to request quotes and access emergency fueling services.\nThe company, a sister business of Prime Equipment Solutions, serves construction, heavy civil, industrial and mission-critical projects.\nThe company did not disclose the number of vehicles added to its fleet, the investment involved or which neighboring states it plans to enter.\nWhy it matters: The negotiations could reduce cargo theft risks and costly disruptions along critical freight corridors connecting Mexican manufacturers with U.S. markets.",
